.\" Public Domain.  After rx.4, for the diskettes of a PC.
.\"
.\"	@(#)fl.4	1.1 (2.11BSD) 2026/11/25
.\"
.TH FL 4 "November 25, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
fl \- PC diskettes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
.nf
major device number(s):
	raw: 11
	block: 3
minor device encoding:
	the drive: <fl_drive>
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I fl
device provides access to the two diskette drives of a PC/AT's
diskette controller (an NEC 765 at port 0x3F0, with interrupt request 6
and DMA channel 2).
A drive is 3.5-inch or 5.25-inch, as the machine's setup program
recorded it in the clock chip's memory.
Diskettes are double-sided with 80 cylinders and sectors of 512 bytes.
.PP
The standard device names begin with ``fl'' followed by
the drive number, 0 or 1.
The character ? stands here for a drive number.
.PP
The block files access the diskette via the system's normal
buffering mechanism and may be read and written without regard to
physical disk records.  There is also a `raw' interface
which provides for direct transmission between the diskette
and the user's read or write buffer.
The names of the raw files
conventionally begin with an extra `r.'
In raw I/O counts and
.I seek
offsets must be a multiple of 512 bytes.
.PP
In addition to normal (`block' and `raw') i/o, the driver supports
formatting of diskettes.
.SH "DISK SUPPORT"
The
.I fl
driver does not support pseudo-disks (partitions) and a diskette has
no label of its own.  Each file
.I fl?
refers to the entire diskette as a single sequentially addressed
file.
.PP
A drive takes these diskettes:
.PP
.nf
.ta .5i +\w'3.5-inch high density  'u +\w'sectors/track  'u +\w'sectors  'u
	in a drive that is	sectors/track	sectors	disktab
	3.5-inch, either density	9	1440	fl720
	3.5-inch high density	18	2880	fl1440
	5.25-inch high density	15	2400	fl1200
.DT
.fi
.PP
Diskettes of 360 kilobytes are not supported.
What the diskette in a drive is, the driver finds when the drive is
first read or written after it was last closed, by reading the header
of a sector at the rate of each kind the drive takes in turn, the
largest first.
.I Ioctl
DIOCGDINFO is answered with a label for the kind found, of one
partition, the whole diskette, so that
.IR newfs (8)
needs no disk type.
.PP
The commands
.nf
.RS
% flformat /dev/rfl0
% newfs /dev/rfl0
.RE
.fi
format and make a file system on the diskette in drive 0, of 1.44
megabytes in a 3.5-inch drive;
.B "flformat \-f 720"
formats a double density one.
.IR Tar (1)
and
.IR dump (8)
may write to the raw file directly.
.PP
A diskette with a file system that has
.I /boot
and a kernel in its root directory, and the boot block in its first
sector, starts the system:
.nf
.RS
% dd if=/mdec/fluboot of=/dev/rfl0 count=1
.RE
.fi
The kernel so loaded takes its root file system from a hard disk, a
diskette having no room for a root file system and a swap area.
.PP
An
.IR ioctl (2)
call is available to format a diskette:
.RS
.nf
.ft B
#include <i286at/flreg.h>
int kb;
ioctl(fildes, FLIOC_FORMAT, &kb)
.ft R
.fi
.RE
The diskette is formatted as the kind the drive takes of
.I kb
kilobytes, or with 0, as the largest.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev/rfl[01]  'u
/dev/fl[01]	block files
.br
/dev/rfl[01]	raw files
.br
/mdec/fluboot	boot block
.DT
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rx(4),
disktab(5),
flformat(8),
newfs(8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\fBfl%d: hard error sn%d (sector %d) st0=%b st1=%b\fR.  An unrecoverable
error occurred during transfer of the specified sector of the specified
diskette.  The contents of the controller's first two status registers
are also printed symbolically with bits decoded.
The transfer was tried five times.
.PP
\fBfl%d: format unknown st0=%b st1=%b\fR.  No sector's header could be
read at the rate of any kind of diskette the drive takes: the diskette
is not formatted, or is of another kind.
.PP
\fBfl%d: not ready\fR.  The controller did not answer within three
seconds, usually because no diskette is in the drive.
.SH BUGS
Formatting does not read back what it wrote.
.PP
A diskette changed while its file system is mounted, or while the drive
is otherwise open, is not noticed.
